|  created method factorial(int)

jshell> factorial(10)
$5 ==> 3628800
notice when we used it the 5$ sign is actually entertaining, since we didn't create a variable to
"save " our requested result, jshell created one for us!

The jshell is useful for understanding java syntax. (At least at the beginning)


Casting for primitive variables on java - in case you want to do it you need to mention the
type of convertion. once you do it, For example:
double d = 5.3;
int n = (int) d; (it rounds it down)
this means you wanted to cast the double to int.
Notice that dividing int's results in rounding down to the number.
now dividing 2 different variables the result will always be the most complex variable.
so int/double (or vice verse) the result will be double.
short/int will be int and etc...